From af235c79a210f20da991ec01c441cf670261b277 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GitHub Actions <action@github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:54:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] attachments: publish_draft, an orphan-branch publish, plus
 the two probe corrections
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

#110 measured that nothing in a run can push an orphan branch: `git switch` and
`git push` are refused by `shell_guard`, and `commit_and_push` names a branch from
the work-branch convention and can build no other. The only route left is the one
this server already takes — the process that holds the credential runs the tool —
so `publish_draft` runs `git` from inside the server.

The commit is built from objects rather than checked out: `hash-object -w` per
draft, `update-index --index-info -z` into an index of its own, `commit-tree`,
and one push of a raw refspec. A branch switch in the run's checkout would be a
way to lose uncommitted work that has nothing to do with the call, and the
`GIT_INDEX_FILE` for the temporary index is set on the command rather than on
`process.env` so it cannot leak into a later tool call's `git`.

Refused: the remote's default branch (an orphan branch is safe because it is not
the reviewed one) and the branch the run has checked out (that is the pull
request). Publishing onto a branch that does not exist creates a root commit;
onto one that does creates a fast-forward, so documents accumulate history.

Two probe corrections, both from #109's measurements. `routing` gets an optional
`name`, because an empty one is answered 400 `Invalid name for request` before the
endpoint ever routes — that 400 is now reported as having measured nothing about
routing rather than as unexpected, and the default call is unchanged. `oversize`'s
404 is rewritten: it measured 404 `Not Found` and nothing stored, not 413, and the
old text read it as a permission answer that the same session's `endpoint` 400 had
already ruled out. What a 404 cannot separate — the size from the credential's kind
at the body stage — is stated in the text rather than implied.

No real push and no upload happen here: `publish_draft` needs the checkout's own
credential, so the far-end call is a person's after a merge, the same boundary the
probe already has. What runs on this pull request is 22 tests against a temporary
remote, which is what `publish_draft` actually does. `atoma validate
--with-live-tools` passes and the server advertises `probe` and `publish_draft`.
